<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Sioux Reservation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1868–1889</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Sioux Reservation was the territory reserved for the Lakota and other Sioux by the Treaty of Fort Laramie of 1868, covering present-day South Dakota west of the Missouri River, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> took the Black Hills in 1877 after the Great Sioux War, and in 1889 Congress broke up the remaining land into six smaller reservations, opening much of it to settlers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-sioux-reservation.jpg" alt="Great Sioux Reservation" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Sack · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sack_GreatSiouxNation1868.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
